## Using the detector

Add `-race` to any build-style command:

	go run   -race .
	go test  -race ./...
	go build -race .     # instrumented binary for staging/canary

That's the whole interface. The skill is in reading what comes back.

: Emphasize go build -race — people forget you can ship an instrumented binary to a canary or staging replica and catch races under real traffic.

## The demo program

.code demo/counter.go /^type incrementor/,/^}/

.code demo/counter.go /^func \(inc \*incrementor\) increment/,/^}/

: This is 01-race-detector/demo/counter.go, unmodified. Two goroutines call increment concurrently.
:
: Run it without -race first: the count is wrong, but nothing complains. Then run it with -race.
:
: Demo diffs apply in sequence from the repo root: mutex.diff, trace.diff, fix-mutex.diff, atomic.diff. Reset with: git checkout -- 01-race-detector/demo/counter.go

## How it works — the 2-minute version

- Built on **ThreadSanitizer**. Every memory access and every synchronization
  operation is instrumented.
- Every sync op is a clock tick: mutex lock/unlock, channel send/receive,
  `WaitGroup.Wait`, atomics, goroutine start.
- Using **vector clocks**, the runtime tracks the **happens-before** relation
  defined by the [Go memory model](https://go.dev/ref/mem).
- If two conflicting accesses are not ordered by happens-before, that's a data
  race — *even if the accesses didn't physically collide on this run.*

: That last clause is the one that surprises people. Don't skip it — it sets up the next slide, and it's why the detector is so much better than 'run it a thousand times and hope'.

## Why it reports races you never saw happen

.html figures/happens-before.html

: The detector isn't watching for two accesses to physically overlap in time. It's asking whether anything in the memory model ordered them.
:
: No edge means race, even if this particular run happened to space the accesses a second apart.

## No false positives

If `-race` reports a race, **it is a real race.**

Don't rationalize it away — "that's just a stats counter", "the window is tiny",
"we've shipped it for years."

The compiler and the CPU are allowed to miscompile racy code in surprising ways.

: Be emphatic. The most common failure mode in the room is an engineer talking themselves out of a real report.
:
: There is no such thing as a benign data race in Go's memory model.

## False negatives *are* possible

The detector only sees code that actually **executes**, and only flags accesses
that actually happen concurrently without ordering **during that run**.

- An untested code path produces no report.
- A schedule where the two goroutines never overlap produces no report.
- Coverage depends on exercising realistic concurrent workloads.

A green build is not proof of a race-free program.

: This is the honest counterweight to the previous slide. Both matter: trust every report you get, but don't read silence as safety.

## Anatomy of a race report

.html figures/race-report.html

The address matches *within* a run, not across runs — Go 1.26 randomizes the
heap base address at startup.

: This is the core skill of this hour. Walk it top to bottom, live.
:
: The heap randomization point matters because people try to compare addresses between two runs, or paste an address into a bug report as if it were stable. It's a security feature (harder to predict addresses when using cgo); disable with GOEXPERIMENT=norandomizedheapbase64 if you ever need to.
:
: 1. The two accesses. The first stack is the access that triggered the report; 'Previous write/read' is the earlier, conflicting access. At least one of the two is always a write. Same address, two goroutines, no ordering — that's the race.
:
: 2. Read vs write matters — next slide.
:
: 3. Goroutine creation sites tell you which go statement launched each party, often the fastest way to identify which worker or monitor is involved when both stacks look identical.

## Read vs write matters

- **Write / write** races corrupt data. Two goroutines write; the result can be
  a value neither one ever wrote.
- **Read / write** races return torn or stale values.
- Multi-word values are the danger zone: `time.Time`, maps, slices, interfaces,
  strings — all more than one word, all observable **half-written**.
- A half-written interface is a crash: type word from one value, data word from
  another.

: The interface example usually gets a reaction. It explains panics that look impossible from reading the source — which is exactly the kind of bug people waste days on.

## Fix the shared state, not each report

One warning per racy **pair**.

A single root cause — one unprotected struct — commonly produces many reports.

Count the distinct addresses, not the distinct warnings.

: Students in the exercises will see a wall of reports and start fixing line by line. Head that off now.

