.html figures/cost.html

: Overhead is the reason people give for not running it. Give them the real numbers so they can make the real tradeoff.
:
: Status 66 is what fails your build — worth calling out explicitly.

## Tuning with GORACE

	GORACE="halt_on_error=1"          go run -race .        # stop at the first race
	GORACE="log_path=/tmp/race"       go test -race ./...   # write reports to files
	GORACE="strip_path_prefix=$PWD/"  go run -race .        # shorter paths in reports

`halt_on_error=1` is what you want when one root cause is generating a flood.

`strip_path_prefix` is what makes reports fit on a slide — and in a terminal.

: By default the report goes to stderr and the program keeps running, which is why a single run can produce a wall of output.

## Races in tests and CI

- Run `go test -race ./...` in CI. **Non-negotiable** for concurrent code — the
  cheapest place to catch a race, and the exit code fails the build.
- The detector only finds races your tests *provoke*. Add tests that exercise
  concurrency deliberately: multiple goroutines hammering the API.
- `-race` lowers throughput. Set timeouts accordingly — a test that passes at
  1× may time out at 10×.

: This is the highest-leverage takeaway of the hour for most of the room: turn it on in CI today.

## testing/synctest — stable since Go 1.25

Runs a test in a **bubble** with a fake clock: concurrent tests become fast and
deterministic.

- The race detector understands synctest's synchronization, so `synctest` +
  `-race` is a powerful combination for testing concurrent code.
- A 2.4-second timing test runs in a bubble in ~0ms.

The bonus lab, `exercises/ex4-testing/`, is a hands-on tour of both points: a
green test suite hiding a race from CI, and that 2.4-second test rewritten.

: Homework by default. If you're ahead of the clock this is fully scripted and runs in 15–20 minutes.

## sync.WaitGroup.Go — Go 1.25

Replaces the error-prone `Add`/`Done` dance. You'll see it in the demo.

.html figures/waitgroup-go.html

And you don't have to do it by hand — `go fix` rewrites it for you:

	go fix ./...        # the waitgroupgo modernizer

: Fewer places to forget a Done() and hang forever. Small quality-of-life change, but it removes an entire category of 'why is this test hanging' bugs.
:
: go fix was completely rebuilt in Go 1.26 and is now the home of Go's modernizers — a push-button way to update a codebase to current idioms. The analyzer that does this rewrite is called `waitgroup` in 1.26 and was renamed `waitgroupgo` in 1.27.
:
: It builds on the same analysis framework as go vet, so the same analyzers that report in vet can apply fixes in go fix.

## What the race detector won't catch

- Races on code paths that **never executed** during the run.
- Races the schedule happened to order on this run — run tests repeatedly and
  under load; `-count=` helps.
- **Logical races** that aren't data races: check-then-act bugs where every
  individual access is synchronized but the *sequence* isn't atomic.
- **Deadlocks and goroutine leaks** — nothing is racing, nothing is moving.

: The logical-race bullet is the bridge to exercise 3 (banking): race-detector-clean and still wrong. Set it up here so the exercise lands.
:
: The last bullet is the bridge to Parts II and III.

## Choosing the fix

.html figures/fix-menu.html

: The demo walks counter.go through mutex.diff, trace.diff, fix-mutex.diff, atomic.diff in sequence.
:
: The point to land: atomic is not 'the fast mutex'. It's correct only when the whole invariant fits in one operation — which is exactly what exercise 3 violates.

## Your turn

- **Exercise 1 — Stats Tracker.** Invisible counter races. The report is clean
  and the fix is short; start here to build the reading habit.
- **Exercise 2 — Cache & Metrics.** Flaky map crashes. Concurrent map access,
  the runtime's own fatal error, and why that isn't always a race report.
- **Exercise 3 — Banking.** "Looks synchronized but isn't." Every access is
  locked; the sequence still isn't atomic. The hardest of the three.

: Every Part I race fires under -race on every run — these are deterministic. If something behaves oddly it's likely a stale edit: git checkout -- <dir> and rerun.
